From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 08:22:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: derive the display config from the driver once per
 session.

The registry copy is a cache of what some earlier session saw, and reading it
back was taken as evidence that it still describes this one.  It often does
not: the same prefix run against a different display, a remote session replaced
by the local panel and back again at another size, a desktop resized while
nothing was running.  Nothing invalidates the cache in any of those cases,
because the driver only signals a change to processes that were already there
to hear it.

A session that starts afterwards then sizes its windows from the old screen,
which is not a display problem anyone recognises as one -- the window is simply
the wrong size and will not go bigger.

Ask the driver once, when the window station's serial is still at the one it
was created with and so nobody in this session has published a monitor set yet;
every read after that comes from the cache as before.  Keying it on the process
instead would put a full enumeration, vulkan GPUs and all, in front of every
program's first window: measured here, the first process in a session
enumerates and the second does not.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit e924737ad98fdcf3f67ea1d9a5e686bd35333c26)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
index 3d4eaffc05d..7880e75318a 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
@@ -2917,6 +2917,22 @@ static BOOL lock_display_devices( BOOL force )
         return TRUE;
     }
 
+    /* A session that has not read the config yet may not be looking at the
+     * screen the stored one was written for: the same prefix run against a
+     * different display, a remote session replaced by the local panel, a
+     * desktop resized while nothing was running.  The registry is a cache of
+     * what some earlier session saw, and reading it back is no evidence that
+     * it still describes this one -- a window sized from it comes out the
+     * wrong size and cannot be resized past it, which is not a display problem
+     * anyone recognises as one.  Derive it from the driver once per session
+     * and let the cache serve every read after that.
+     *
+     * "Once per session" is the window station's serial, not this process's:
+     * it starts at one and moves the first time anyone publishes a monitor
+     * set, so a serial still at one means nobody in this session has looked
+     * yet.  Keying it on the process would put a full enumeration, vulkan GPUs
+     * and all, in front of every program's first window. */
+    if (serial <= 1) force = TRUE;
     if (!force && !update_display_cache_from_registry( serial )) force = TRUE;
     if (force)
     {
